# Website Health Monitor — Cursor rules

The canonical brief is [AGENTS.md](../../AGENTS.md). Read it before editing; this
file is the short operational version.

## Stack and shape

- Python 3.11+ / FastAPI / `httpx` / stdlib `sqlite3` / static HTML+CSS+JS dashboard.
- One application process. The scheduler, per-site locks, and webhook dispatcher
  are in-process and must stay that way.
- Module boundaries: `checks` measure reality, `policy` interprets evidence
  (no I/O), `database` persists, `monitoring` runs one cycle, `main` serves.

## Commands

- `make check` — full gate (ruff check, ruff format --check, metadata verify,
  pytest with a 90% coverage floor). Run it before saying work is done.
- `make test`, `make format`, `make verify`, `make run`,
  `make backup DEST=backups/health.db`.

## Rules

- Never weaken lint rules, coverage thresholds, or CI workflows to pass a change.
- Never add authentication, cloud services, Kubernetes, Redis, ORMs, container
  requirements, or frontend build steps; this is an intentional MVP boundary.
- Never change the default loopback bind (`WHM_HOST=127.0.0.1`). There is no
  authentication, so public exposure is unsafe and out of scope.
- Never log or persist secrets, webhook URLs, credentials, or HTTP response
  bodies; URLs with credentials are rejected on input.
- Keep `app/policy.py` deterministic and I/O-free, and always preserve
  `reason.code`, messages, and check evidence in evaluations.
- Isolate check failures: one broken check must not cancel its siblings.
- Timestamps are UTC ISO-8601. A monitored URL is immutable once it has history.
- Bump `app/__init__.py`, `pyproject.toml`, and the newest `CHANGELOG.md` heading
  together; `make verify` enforces this.
- Update the matching document in `docs/` whenever behavior or configuration
  changes.

## Testing style

Use deterministic fakes, injected clocks, `httpx.MockTransport`, injected DNS
resolvers, and injected TLS probes. No live network, no sleeps, no real
certificates, no credentials. Cover the negative path and threshold boundaries.
